---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "Overview", href: "#overview", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Worker Model", href: "#worker-model", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Remote Control", href: "#remote-control", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Remote Attach", href: "#remote-attach", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="TUI"
  subtitle="Terminal UI entrypoint and worker model in packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>The TUI command is the default CLI route and supports local or server-backed sessions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="worker-model" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Worker Model</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI spawns a worker process and communicates over an RPC channel. When server flags are
      present, the TUI starts an HTTP server and connects via events.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remote-control" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Remote Control</h2>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Slash commands">
      <p>
        Use <code>/remote-control</code> (or alias <code>/rc</code>) inside a TUI session to open the
        Remote Control dialog. Legacy alias <code>/remote</code> is still supported.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      If a remote session already exists for the running instance, Nikcli reuses it and keeps the QR
      URL in sync. Remote defaults can be configured under <code>config.remote</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remote-attach" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Remote Attach</h2>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Attach">
      <p><code>nikcli attach &lt;url&gt;</code> connects a local TUI to a remote server.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
